Yes ✅ — src/ is the root folder of your NestJS application code.

When we say “root” here, we mean:

The root of your application source code, not the root of the entire repository.

🧠 In Your Case (Workspace Setup)

Your real project root is:

slardar-sniper/


But your Nest app likely lives in:

slardar-sniper/apps/api/


So the actual structure would be:

slardar-sniper/
 ├── apps/
 │    └── api/
 │         ├── src/   👈 this is your NestJS source root
 │         ├── package.json
 │         ├── tsconfig.json
 │         └── ...
 ├── Dockerfile
 ├── docker-compose.yaml
 ├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
 └── package.json


So yes:

apps/api/src/


is your application source root.

🏗 Final Real Structure (Complete Picture)
slardar-sniper/
 ├── apps/
 │    └── api/
 │         ├── src/
 │         │    ├── modules/
 │         │    │    ├── user/
 │         │    │    ├── auth/
 │         │    ├── common/
 │         │    ├── config/
 │         │    ├── main.ts
 │         │    └── app.module.ts
 │         ├── package.json
 │         └── tsconfig.json
 ├── Dockerfile
 ├── docker-compose.yaml
 ├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
 └── package.json

🧠 Why Nest Uses src/

Nest CLI creates:

src/
  main.ts
  app.module.ts


Because:

TypeScript compiles from src/

Output goes to dist/

Keeps source and compiled code separated

🎯 Important Concept
Folder	Meaning
slardar-sniper/	Monorepo root
apps/api/	One NestJS application
apps/api/src/	Application source root
dist/	Compiled output
✅ Short Answer

Yes — src/ is the root folder of your NestJS app.

But in a monorepo, it is not the repository root — it is the app’s source root.

